WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.369
Source ingestion is the first architectural control point in private RAG.

00:00:04.369 --> 00:00:08.639
The system may ingest files, wikis, knowledge bases, tickets, source repositories,

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:12.908
policy libraries, transcripts, shared drives, structured records, or other internal sources.

00:00:12.908 --> 00:00:17.954
Each source arrives with its own owner, permission model, quality level, update pattern,

00:00:17.954 --> 00:00:19.506
retention expectation, and sensitivity.

00:00:19.506 --> 00:00:25.328
A private RAG pipeline should not flatten those differences into one uncontrolled pile of text.

00:00:25.328 --> 00:00:29.986
During ingestion, the system should preserve the source system, document owner, classification,

00:00:29.986 --> 00:00:33.867
access rules, ingestion date, modified date, retention status, lifecycle state,

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:35.031
and deletion expectations.

00:00:35.031 --> 00:00:36.972
Those details are not paperwork.

00:00:36.972 --> 00:00:40.853
They are the data controls that later retrieval depends on.

00:00:40.853 --> 00:00:42.794
Boundaries also exist inside content.

00:00:42.794 --> 00:00:46.675
A document may contain sections, tables, appendices, exceptions, regional instructions,

00:00:46.675 --> 00:00:48.616
tenant-specific clauses, or archived material.

00:00:48.616 --> 00:00:52.497
A shared repository may mix public guidance with restricted notes.

00:00:52.497 --> 00:00:55.990
A policy library may contain current and retired versions.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:01:00.648
Ingestion should keep those distinctions visible enough for chunking, filtering, and review.

00:01:00.648 --> 00:01:04.917
Data normalization is useful when it makes content searchable and consistent.

00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:08.410
Boundary loss is dangerous when it removes ownership, classification,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.904
or the meaning of where a statement came from.

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:16.561
The goal is to prepare content for retrieval without stripping away the

00:01:16.561 --> 00:01:19.278
rules that made the original source governable.

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:21.995
Private RAG starts by respecting source systems.

00:01:21.995 --> 00:01:25.100
If a document is expired, confidential, tenant-specific, archived,

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.757
or restricted before ingestion, it should remain recognizable as such after ingestion.

00:01:29.757 --> 00:01:33.251
Carrying that meaning forward makes retrieval safer, review easier,

00:01:33.251 --> 00:01:35.191
and deletion workflows more reliable.

00:01:35.191 --> 00:01:37.520
Treat ingestion as a governed handoff.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.566
Before content enters the RAG pipeline, the team should know who approved it,

00:01:42.566 --> 00:01:45.282
what connector touched it, what transformations occurred,

00:01:45.282 --> 00:01:49.940
and what evidence would be needed to remove or correct it later.
